title: Breast cancer risk and hereditary predisposition
type: text/vnd.tiddlywiki

* ''Testing changes care:'' Offer BRCA1/2 testing for newly diagnosed breast cancer at age ≤65.
** Test older patients when history, ancestry, male sex, triple-negative disease, or PARP eligibility warrants.
** Unaffected relatives with suggestive pedigrees merit genetic counseling; test affected relatives first when feasible.
* ''Recognize patterns:'' BRCA1 favors triple-negative disease; BRCA2 favors hormone-sensitive disease and male breast cancer.
** PALB2: high-penetrance breast susceptibility, pancreatic association; TP53: Li-Fraumeni, very early breast cancer, sarcomas.
** PTEN: Cowden, macrocephaly, thyroid/endometrial cancers; CDH1: lobular breast/diffuse gastric cancers.
** STK11: Peutz-Jeghers; ATM and [[CHEK2|CHEK2 mutation: implications and management]] generally confer moderate penetrance.
* ''Actionability:'' Pathogenic/likely pathogenic variants guide surveillance, surgical counseling, and family cascade testing.
** TP53 favors avoiding radiation when feasible; germline BRCA1/2 may enable PARP inhibitors.
*** Breast indications require appropriate stage/risk and HER2-negative disease.
** Variants of uncertain significance never independently justify treatment or preventive surgery.
* ''Risk context:'' US incidence rises, particularly below 50; mortality falls, but Black women fare worse.
** Female sex, age, family history, prolonged reproductive estrogen exposure, and young-age chest irradiation increase risk.
** Combined menopausal hormones, postmenopausal obesity, alcohol, and density increase risk; activity is inversely associated.
* See [[screening and prevention|Breast oncology]] for risk-based choices; associations do not establish intervention benefit.

Sources: ASCO SEP 2026, ch.10 “Breast Cancer,” pp.431–436; [[ASCO–SSO 2024|https://doi.org/10.1200/JCO.23.02225]]; NCCN Genetic/Familial High-Risk Assessment: Breast, Ovarian, Pancreatic, and Prostate v1.2027, CRIT-2; Discussion MS-9–10.
